# Scoping Renovate to Specific Packages

`app/requirements.txt` sets `--index-url` to the private Lightwell registry
(`packages.redhat.com/lightwell/python/remediated/simple`), which only hosts
a subset of packages (currently `PyYAML` and `Jinja2`). Renovate treats
`--index-url` as the primary registry for every package in that file, so any
package not on the Lightwell registry (e.g. `Flask`, `gunicorn`, `Pygments`)
gets a 404 and fails lookup, even with `--extra-index-url` pointing at PyPI.

To keep Renovate scoped to only the packages Lightwell actually remediates,
disable the manager by default and re-enable it per-package with
`matchPackageNames`. Renovate applies `packageRules` in order and later
matching rules override earlier ones, so list the disable rule first:

```json
"packageRules": [
  {
    "matchManagers": ["pip_requirements"],
    "enabled": false
  },
  {
    "matchManagers": ["pip_requirements"],
    "matchDatasources": ["pypi"],
    "matchPackageNames": ["PyYAML", "Jinja2"],
    "enabled": true,
    "registryUrls": [
      "https://packages.redhat.com/lightwell/python/remediated/simple",
      "https://pypi.org/simple/"
    ]
  }
]
```

Do not "fix" 404s by adding a catch-all `registryUrls` fallback to PyPI for
every package -- that reintroduces update PRs (e.g. `pytest`, `Flask`) that
are out of scope for the Lightwell remediation workflow. When adding a new
package to the Lightwell registry, add its name to `matchPackageNames` in the
enable rule rather than relaxing the default-disabled rule.
